[{"problem": "A triangle is drawn on transparent paper. Without using any tools, construct the center of its inscribed circle.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how to construct the center of the inscribed circle (incenter) of a triangle drawn on transparent paper without using any tools. Hmm, let's start by recalling what the incenter is. The incenter is the point where the angle bisectors of the triangle intersect, right? It's also the center of the circle that touches all three sides of the triangle. But how do I construct that without any tools like a ruler or compass?\n\nWait, maybe I can use folding or some sort of symmetry. If the paper is transparent, maybe I can fold the paper along the angle bisectors. But how do I find the angle bisectors just by folding? Let me think.\n\nFirst, angle bisector divides the angle into two equal parts. If I can fold the paper such that the two sides of an angle align, the crease should be the angle bisector. For example, take one vertex of the triangle, say vertex A. If I fold the paper so that side AB aligns with side AC, the fold line would be the angle bisector of angle A. Similarly, I can do this for another angle, say angle B. The intersection of these two folds should be the incenter. Then I can verify by checking the third angle bisector as well.\n\nBut wait, how do I ensure that the sides align correctly when folding? Let me visualize. Suppose the triangle is ABC. To find the angle bisector of angle A, I need to bring side AB to coincide with side AC. When folding, if I can make the edges AB and AC lie on top of each other, the fold line should pass through vertex A and bisect the angle. Then the crease formed will be the bisector. Similarly for angle B and angle C. The point where all three bisectors intersect is the incenter.\n\nBut folding might be tricky without any tools. Maybe I need to use another method. Alternatively, since the incenter is equidistant from all sides, perhaps I can find a point that is equidistant from all three sides by folding. But how?\n\nAnother approach: The incenter is located at the intersection of the angle bisectors. So if I can create two angle bisectors by folding, their intersection is the incenter. Let's try step by step.\n\n1. Take the triangle ABC on transparent paper.\n2. Fold vertex A such that side AB folds onto side AC. The crease will be the angle bisector of angle A. Let's call this fold line AD.\n3. Repeat the process for vertex B: fold side BA onto side BC. The crease will be the angle bisector of angle B, let's call it BE.\n4. The intersection point of AD and BE should be the incenter. To confirm, I can check if the third angle bisector (from angle C) also passes through this point.\n\nBut when folding, how exactly do I make sure the sides align? Let me detail each fold.\n\nFor angle A: Bring vertex B to vertex C by folding along the angle bisector? Wait, no. To bisect angle A, I need to have the two sides AB and AC overlap. So maybe I should align AB with AC. If I fold the paper such that point B is folded onto point C, but that might not necessarily bisect angle A unless AB and AC are equal. Wait, maybe not. Let's think differently.\n\nAlternatively, to bisect angle A, I can fold the paper so that side AB is folded onto side AC. The crease will then lie along the angle bisector. To do this, I can imagine folding the paper such that the line AB coincides with AC; the fold line would then be the bisector. Similarly for other angles.\n\nBut how to perform this fold practically? Let's take angle A. If I hold the paper and fold it such that the edge AB is placed exactly over the edge AC, the crease formed will be the bisector of angle A. Similarly, doing this for angle B will give another bisector. The intersection of these two creases is the incenter.\n\nAlternatively, if folding is difficult, maybe using the midpoint of the sides? Wait, the incenter isn't necessarily the centroid, which is the intersection of medians. So that's different. The centroid is found by connecting midpoints, but the incenter is angle bisectors.\n\nAnother thought: The incenter is equidistant from all sides. If I can somehow create folds that are equidistant from two sides, their intersection would be the incenter. For instance, a fold that is parallel to one side but that's not necessarily helpful.\n\nWait, but if I can create a fold that is the angle bisector, which by definition is the set of points equidistant from the two sides forming the angle. So, if I fold along the angle bisector of angle A, that crease is the locus of points equidistant from AB and AC. Similarly, the angle bisector of angle B is equidistant from BA and BC. Their intersection is equidistant from all three sides, hence the incenter.\n\nTherefore, the method would be:\n\n1. Fold the triangle along the angle bisector of angle A. This can be done by aligning sides AB and AC and making a crease.\n2. Fold the triangle along the angle bisector of angle B similarly.\n3. The intersection point of these two creases is the incenter.\n4. To verify, fold along the angle bisector of angle C and check if it also passes through the same point.\n\nBut how to ensure the alignment when folding AB and AC? Maybe by bringing vertex B to vertex C? Wait, if I fold vertex B onto vertex C, the crease would be the perpendicular bisector of BC, which is not necessarily the angle bisector. So that's different.\n\nWait, perhaps aligning the sides AB and AC. If I fold the paper such that side AB lies directly on top of side AC, the crease will pass through vertex A and bisect the angle. This is because when the two sides are aligned, the fold line will symmetrically divide the angle into two equal parts.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So the key is to fold the sides onto each other, not the vertices. So for angle A, bring side AB to coincide with side AC. The fold that allows this alignment will be the angle bisector. Similarly for angle B.\n\nTherefore, the steps would be:\n\n1. For each angle, fold the paper such that the two adjacent sides are aligned. Each fold creates an angle bisector.\n2. The intersection point of any two angle bisectors is the incenter.\n\nNow, let's check if this works. Suppose triangle ABC is any triangle. When we fold AB onto AC, the crease is the angle bisector of angle A. Similarly, folding BC onto BA gives the angle bisector of angle B. Their intersection is the incenter. Since all three angle bisectors meet at the incenter, two should suffice to locate it, and the third can confirm.\n\nPotential challenges: Ensuring that when folding, the sides are aligned correctly. For example, if the triangle is not isosceles, aligning AB and AC might require careful folding to ensure they overlap properly. But since the paper is transparent, perhaps aligning the edges precisely by sight is possible.\n\nAnother check: The incenter should be equidistant from all sides. Once located, if I fold perpendiculars from the incenter to each side, they should all be equal. But without measuring tools, how can I confirm? Maybe by folding the incenter onto each side and seeing if the distance (length of the fold) is the same? But that's more complex.\n\nAlternatively, since the incenter is the intersection of angle bisectors, as long as the angle bisectors are correctly folded, their intersection must be the incenter.\n\nTherefore, the construction method is folding each angle bisector by aligning the two sides meeting at that angle, then finding their intersection.\n\nSo the step-by-step process would be:\n\n1. Start with triangle ABC on transparent paper.\n2. To construct the angle bisector of angle A:\n   - Fold the paper such that side AB is folded onto side AC. Ensure that the fold passes through vertex A.\n   - The crease formed is the angle bisector of angle A. Let\u2019s call this fold line AD.\n3. Repeat for angle B:\n   - Fold the paper such that side BC is folded onto side BA. The crease is the angle bisector of angle B, fold line BE.\n4. The intersection point of AD and BE is the incenter, point I.\n5. Optionally, fold the angle bisector of angle C (aligning CA with CB) to confirm it also passes through point I.\n\nThis method relies on accurately folding the sides onto each other to create the angle bisectors. Since the paper is transparent, aligning the sides should be feasible by matching their edges visually. Once two angle bisectors are folded, their intersection gives the incenter. The third bisector serves as a verification step to ensure accuracy.\n\nPossible sources of error: If the sides are not aligned precisely when folding, the angle bisector crease might not be accurate, leading to an incorrect incenter. Therefore, careful alignment is necessary. Additionally, the transparency of the paper helps in aligning the edges correctly by allowing the user to see both sides simultaneously during the fold.\n\nAnother consideration: If the triangle is too obtuse or acute, the folding might be more challenging, but the principle remains the same. The angle bisectors are always found by folding the two sides to coincide, regardless of the triangle's type.\n\nIn summary, the incenter can be constructed by folding two angle bisectors through aligning the respective sides of the triangle and finding their intersection. This method uses the properties of angle bisectors and the transparency of the paper to achieve the construction without traditional tools.", "deepseek_solution": "To construct the incenter of a triangle on transparent paper without tools, follow these steps using folding to create angle bisectors:\n\n1. **Angle Bisector of Vertex A:**\n   - Fold the paper so side **AB** aligns precisely with side **AC**, ensuring the fold passes through vertex **A**. The crease formed is the angle bisector of \u2220A.\n\n2. **Angle Bisector of Vertex B:**\n   - Fold the paper such that side **BA** aligns with side **BC**, creating a crease through vertex **B**. This is the angle bisector of \u2220B.\n\n3. **Locate the Incenter:**\n   - The intersection point of the two creases (angle bisectors from steps 1 and 2) is the **incenter** (I), the center of the inscribed circle.\n\n4. **Verification (Optional):**\n   - Fold the angle bisector of \u2220C by aligning sides **CA** and **CB**. Confirm the crease passes through point **I**, ensuring accuracy.\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe incenter is found at the intersection of the angle bisectors constructed by folding adjacent sides onto each other.  \n\\boxed{I}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the Problem**: \n   The goal is to find the center of the inscribed circle (incircle) of a given triangle drawn on transparent paper, without using any instruments.\n\n2. **Concept**: \n   The center of the inscribed circle in a triangle is the point where the internal angle bisectors of the triangle intersect.\n\n3. **Possible Approach**: \n   Leveraging the properties of folding (or symmetry) due to the transparency of the paper to construct the angle bisectors.\n\n4. **Constructing an Angle Bisector via Folding**:\n    1. Identify one vertex of the triangle.\n    2. Fold the paper such that two sides that meet at this vertex coincide, making sure the crease runs through the vertex. This crease is the bisector of the angle at this vertex.\n   \n5. **Repeating for Another Vertex**:\n   Repeat the above step for another vertex of the triangle, creating the second angle bisector.\n\n6. **Intersection of Angle Bisectors**:\n   The point where these two angle bisectors intersect is the center of the inscribed circle. By properties of the triangle, this same point will satisfy the angle bisector condition for the third vertex as well, though it's computationally sufficient to use just two bisectors.\n\n7. **Final Verification**:\n    Ensuring that the folds accurately reflect the symmetry for accurate construction of the bisectors.\n\n8. **Conclusion**:\n   The intersection point is the required center of the inscribed circle.\n   \n\\[\\boxed{\\text{End of Solution}}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The roots of the quadratic equation \\(a^{2} + bx + c = 0\\) are 2011 times greater than the roots of the quadratic equation \\(cx^{2} + dx + a = 0\\). Prove that \\(b^{2} = d^{2}\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to prove that \\( b^2 = d^2 \\) given that the roots of the quadratic equation \\( a^{2} + bx + c = 0 \\) are 2011 times greater than the roots of the quadratic equation \\( cx^{2} + dx + a = 0 \\). Hmm, first, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly.\n\nWait, the first equation is written as \\( a^{2} + bx + c = 0 \\). Wait, that seems a bit odd. Usually, a quadratic equation is of the form \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\). Did they maybe mean \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\)? Because otherwise, if it's \\( a^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), that's a linear equation unless there's a typo. Let me check the original problem again.\n\nThe original problem says: \"The roots of the quadratic equation \\( a^{2} + bx + c = 0 \\) are 2011 times greater than the roots of the quadratic equation \\( cx^{2} + dx + a = 0 \\). Prove that \\( b^{2} = d^{2} \\).\"\n\nHmm, so the first equation is quadratic. If it's quadratic, the coefficient of \\( x^2 \\) must be non-zero. But in \\( a^{2} + bx + c = 0 \\), the coefficient of \\( x^2 \\) is zero, because there's no \\( x^2 \\) term. Wait, that doesn't make sense. Maybe there's a typo here. Let me see. Perhaps the first equation is supposed to be \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), which is standard. Maybe the user mistyped it as \\( a^2 + bx + c = 0 \\). Because otherwise, if it's \\( a^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), that's a linear equation in x, unless \\( a \\) is a variable, but in that case, it's not a quadratic equation. So perhaps there's a typo, and the first equation is meant to be \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\). Let me proceed with that assumption, as otherwise, the problem doesn't make sense.\n\nSo assuming the first equation is \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), and the second equation is \\( cx^2 + dx + a = 0 \\). The roots of the first equation are 2011 times greater than the roots of the second. We need to prove \\( b^2 = d^2 \\).\n\nAlright, let's recall that for a quadratic equation \\( px^2 + qx + r = 0 \\), the sum of the roots is \\( -q/p \\) and the product is \\( r/p \\). So let me denote the roots of the first equation as \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\beta \\), and the roots of the second equation as \\( \\gamma \\) and \\( \\delta \\). According to the problem, each root of the first equation is 2011 times the corresponding root of the second equation. Wait, but quadratic equations have two roots. Does \"2011 times greater\" mean that each root of the first equation is 2011 times the corresponding root of the second equation? Or is there a scaling factor?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the roots of the first equation are 2011 times the roots of the second equation. So if the second equation has roots \\( \\gamma \\) and \\( \\delta \\), then the first equation has roots \\( 2011\\gamma \\) and \\( 2011\\delta \\). That seems more plausible.\n\nLet me proceed with that understanding. So if the first equation has roots 2011 times the roots of the second equation, then the roots of the first equation are \\( 2011\\gamma \\) and \\( 2011\\delta \\), where \\( \\gamma \\) and \\( \\delta \\) are the roots of the second equation.\n\nSo for the first equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), sum of roots is \\( -b/a \\), and product is \\( c/a \\).\n\nSum of roots: \\( 2011\\gamma + 2011\\delta = 2011(\\gamma + \\delta) = -b/a \\).\n\nProduct of roots: \\( (2011\\gamma)(2011\\delta) = 2011^2 \\gamma\\delta = c/a \\).\n\nFor the second equation \\( cx^2 + dx + a = 0 \\), sum of roots is \\( -d/c \\), and product is \\( a/c \\).\n\nSo sum of roots for second equation: \\( \\gamma + \\delta = -d/c \\).\n\nProduct of roots: \\( \\gamma\\delta = a/c \\).\n\nSo let's substitute these into the expressions from the first equation.\n\nFirst, sum of roots of first equation:\n\n2011(\\gamma + \\delta) = 2011(-d/c) = -b/a.\n\nSo:\n\n2011*(-d/c) = -b/a.\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n2011*(d/c) = b/a.\n\nSo 2011d/c = b/a.\n\nTherefore, cross-multiplying:\n\n2011d * a = b * c.\n\nSo 2011 a d = b c. Let's keep that equation (1).\n\nNow, product of roots of first equation:\n\n2011^2 * gamma delta = 2011^2*(a/c) = c/a.\n\nBecause gamma delta = a/c (from the second equation's product of roots).\n\nSo:\n\n2011^2*(a/c) = c/a.\n\nMultiply both sides by c/a:\n\n2011^2*(a/c)*(c/a) = c/a*(c/a).\n\nSimplify left side: 2011^2*1 = (c/a)^2.\n\nTherefore, 2011^2 = (c/a)^2.\n\nTaking square roots:\n\nc/a = \u00b12011.\n\nWait, but 2011 is positive, so c/a is either 2011 or -2011.\n\nSo c = \u00b12011 a.\n\nSo from equation (1): 2011 a d = b c = b*(\u00b12011 a).\n\nSo substituting c = \u00b12011 a into equation (1):\n\n2011 a d = b*(\u00b12011 a).\n\nDivide both sides by 2011 a (assuming a \u2260 0 and 2011 \u2260 0):\n\nd = \u00b1b.\n\nTherefore, d = \u00b1b, so squaring both sides, d^2 = b^2, hence b^2 = d^2. That's what we needed to prove.\n\nWait, let me check if all steps are valid.\n\nFirst, we assumed that the roots of the first equation are 2011 times the roots of the second equation. Then we used Vieta's formulas for both equations. Then substituted the sum and product expressions. Then derived 2011 a d = b c, and c = \u00b12011 a. Then substituted c into the first equation and found that d = \u00b1b, hence d^2 = b^2. Seems okay.\n\nBut let me verify whether the assumption that the roots are scaled by 2011 is correct. The problem says \"2011 times greater\". Depending on interpretation, \"times greater\" can sometimes be ambiguous. In some contexts, people might confuse \"times greater\" with \"times as much\". For example, \"3 times greater\" sometimes is mistakenly used to mean \"3 times as much\", but literally, \"3 times greater\" would mean the original plus 3 times the original, which is 4 times the original. However, in mathematical contexts, especially problems like this, when they say \"the roots are 2011 times greater\", it's more likely that they mean \"each root is multiplied by 2011\", i.e., scaled by 2011. So the roots of the first equation are 2011 times the roots of the second. Therefore, the way I approached it is correct.\n\nBut just to be thorough, suppose the problem had meant that each root of the first equation is 2011 more than the roots of the second equation. Then the relationship would be different. But in that case, the equations would have roots differing by a constant, which complicates things. However, given the problem's phrasing and the fact that scaling roots is a common type of problem, I think my original approach is correct.\n\nAnother check: Let's suppose that the roots of the first equation are 2011 times the roots of the second. Then, if we let the second equation have roots k and m, the first equation has roots 2011k and 2011m. Then, using Vieta's formulas:\n\nFor the first equation:\n\nSum: 2011k + 2011m = 2011(k + m) = -b/a.\n\nProduct: (2011k)(2011m) = 2011^2 km = c/a.\n\nFor the second equation:\n\nSum: k + m = -d/c.\n\nProduct: km = a/c.\n\nSo substituting the sum from the second equation into the first equation's sum gives 2011*(-d/c) = -b/a, which leads to 2011d/c = b/a, hence 2011ad = bc.\n\nSimilarly, substituting the product from the second equation into the first equation's product gives 2011^2*(a/c) = c/a. Then, multiplying both sides by c/a gives 2011^2*(a/c)*(c/a) = (c/a)^2, which simplifies to 2011^2 = (c/a)^2, so c/a = \u00b12011, hence c = \u00b12011a.\n\nThen substituting back into 2011ad = bc, which becomes 2011ad = b*(\u00b12011a). Dividing both sides by 2011a (assuming a \u2260 0) gives d = \u00b1b, hence d^2 = b^2. So this confirms the result.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that \\( b^2 = d^2 \\), as required.\n\nPossible edge cases to consider: What if a = 0? Then the first equation would not be quadratic. But the problem states that the first equation is quadratic, so a must not be zero. Similarly, for the second equation, if c = 0, it would not be quadratic. But since the roots of the first equation are 2011 times the roots of the second equation, the second equation must also be quadratic, so c \u2260 0. Therefore, a and c are non-zero, which justifies dividing by them in the steps above.\n\nAnother check: If c = 2011a, then substituting back into the second equation: \\( cx^2 + dx + a = 2011a x^2 + dx + a = 0 \\). Let's see if that works. Then the product of roots is a/c = a/(2011a) = 1/2011, which matches with the first equation's product of roots being 2011^2*(a/c) = 2011^2*(1/2011) = 2011. Which would be c/a = 2011a/a = 2011. So that's consistent. Similarly, if c = -2011a, then product of roots is a/c = a/(-2011a) = -1/2011, and the first equation's product is 2011^2*(-1/2011) = -2011. Which would be c/a = -2011a/a = -2011, so 2011^2*(a/c) = 2011^2*(1/-2011) = -2011, which matches. So that's consistent as well.\n\nTherefore, all steps hold, and the conclusion is valid.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\( b^2 = d^2 \\) given that the roots of \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) are 2011 times the roots of \\( cx^2 + dx + a = 0 \\), we analyze the relationships between the roots using Vieta's formulas.\n\n1. **Define Roots**:  \n   Let the roots of \\( cx^2 + dx + a = 0 \\) be \\( \\gamma \\) and \\( \\delta \\). Then, the roots of \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) are \\( 2011\\gamma \\) and \\( 2011\\delta \\).\n\n2. **Apply Vieta's Formulas**:  \n   - For \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\):  \n     Sum of roots: \\( 2011(\\gamma + \\delta) = -\\frac{b}{a} \\)  \n     Product of roots: \\( (2011\\gamma)(2011\\delta) = \\frac{c}{a} \\)\n   - For \\( cx^2 + dx + a = 0 \\):  \n     Sum of roots: \\( \\gamma + \\delta = -\\frac{d}{c} \\)  \n     Product of roots: \\( \\gamma\\delta = \\frac{a}{c} \\)\n\n3. **Substitute Sum of Roots**:  \n   Substitute \\( \\gamma + \\delta = -\\frac{d}{c} \\) into the sum of the first equation:  \n   \\[\n   2011\\left(-\\frac{d}{c}\\right) = -\\frac{b}{a} \\implies \\frac{2011d}{c} = \\frac{b}{a} \\implies 2011ad = bc \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute Product of Roots**:  \n   Substitute \\( \\gamma\\delta = \\frac{a}{c} \\) into the product of the first equation:  \n   \\[\n   2011^2 \\cdot \\frac{a}{c} = \\frac{c}{a} \\implies 2011^2 \\cdot \\frac{a^2}{c^2} = 1 \\implies \\frac{c}{a} = \\pm 2011 \\implies c = \\pm 2011a\n   \\]\n\n5. **Relate \\( d \\) and \\( b \\)**:  \n   Substitute \\( c = \\pm 2011a \\) into Equation 1:  \n   \\[\n   2011ad = b(\\pm 2011a) \\implies d = \\pm b\n   \\]\n   Squaring both sides gives:  \n   \\[\n   d^2 = b^2\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nBy systematically relating the coefficients through Vieta's formulas and solving the resulting equations, we conclude that \\( b^2 = d^2 \\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consider the quadratic equation \\( \\mathrm{cx}^{2} + \\mathrm{dx} + \\mathrm{a} = 0 \\). Let the roots of this equation be denoted as \\( x_{1} \\) and \\( x_{2} \\).\n\n2. By Vieta's formulas, we know that \n    \\[\n    x_{1} + x_{2} = -\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{c}} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x_{1} x_{2} = \\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{c}}\n    \\]\n\n3. According to the given condition, the roots of the quadratic equation \\( \\mathrm{a}x^{2} + \\mathrm{b}x + \\mathrm{c} = 0 \\) are \\( 2011x_{1} \\) and \\( 2011x_{2} \\). \n\n4. Applying Vieta's formulas to this new equation, we get\n    \\[\n    2011x_{1} + 2011x_{2} = -\\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}}\n    \\]\n   Substituting the known values from the smaller roots, we get\n    \\[\n    2011(x_{1} + x_{2}) = -\\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}} \\implies 2011 \\left( -\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{c}} \\right) = -\\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}}\n    \\]\n    Thus,\n    \\[\n    -2011 \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{c}} = -\\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}} \\implies \\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}} = 2011 \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{c}}\n    \\]\n    Rearrange this as\n    \\[\n    \\mathrm{b} \\cdot \\mathrm{c} = 2011 \\cdot \\mathrm{d} \\cdot \\mathrm{a}\n    \\]\n\n5. For the product of the roots of the quadratic equation \\( \\mathrm{a}x^{2} + \\mathrm{b}x + \\mathrm{c} = 0 \\), we have\n    \\[\n    (2011x_{1}) (2011x_{2}) = \\frac{\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{a}}\n    \\]\n    Substituting the known product, we get\n    \\[\n    2011^{2} \\cdot x_{1} x_{2} = \\frac{\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{a}} \\implies 2011^{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{c}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{a}}\n    \\]\n    Thus,\n    \\[\n    2011^{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{c}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{a}} \\implies 2011^{2} = \\frac{\\mathrm{c}^{2}}{\\mathrm{a}^{2}}\n    \\]\n\n\n6. Taking the square root on both sides, we have\n    \\[\n    2011 = \\frac{\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{a}}\n    \\]\n    Therefore,\n    \\[\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{d}} = 2011\n    \\]\n   \n7. Since both sides represent the same constant ratio, it implies that \n    \\[\n    \\left( \\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}} \\right)^{2} = \\left( 2011 \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{c}} \\right)^{2} \n    \\]\n    Therefore, we get,\n    \\[\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{b}^{2}}{\\mathrm{a}^{2}} = 2011^{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^{2}}{\\mathrm{c}^{2}}\n    \\]\n    indicating that \n    \\[\n    \\boxed{\\mathrm{b}^{2} = \\mathrm{d}^{2}}\n    \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find all integer solutions \\((a, b)\\) of the equation \\(a^{2} b^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2}\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find all integer solutions (a, b) for the equation a\u00b2b\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2. Hmm, let me start by trying to understand what this equation is saying. It looks like the product of a squared and b squared equals the sum of a squared and b squared. That feels a bit symmetric, so maybe there are solutions where a and b are the same? Let me check that first.\n\nIf a = b, then the equation becomes a\u00b2a\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + a\u00b2, which simplifies to a\u2074 = 2a\u00b2. Dividing both sides by a\u00b2 (assuming a \u2260 0) gives a\u00b2 = 2. But wait, a\u00b2 = 2 means a is either \u221a2 or -\u221a2, which are not integers. So that approach doesn't work. Hmm, maybe there are no solutions where a and b are equal. Or maybe if a or b is zero? Let me check that.\n\nIf a = 0, then the left side becomes 0\u00b2 * b\u00b2 = 0, and the right side becomes 0\u00b2 + b\u00b2 = b\u00b2. So 0 = b\u00b2, which implies b = 0. So (0, 0) is a solution. Similarly, if b = 0, the same logic applies, so (0, 0) is definitely a solution. But maybe there are other solutions where neither a nor b is zero?\n\nAlright, let's assume that a and b are both non-zero integers. Then, the equation is a\u00b2b\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2. Maybe I can rearrange this equation to see if I can factor or find some structure. Let's try moving all terms to one side:\n\na\u00b2b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2 = 0.\n\nHmm, perhaps factor this. Let me see. It's a bit tricky. Let me try adding 1 to both sides to see if that helps:\n\na\u00b2b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2 + 1 = 1.\n\nNow, maybe this can be factored. Let me check:\n\na\u00b2b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2 + 1 = (a\u00b2b\u00b2 - a\u00b2) - (b\u00b2 - 1) = a\u00b2(b\u00b2 - 1) - (b\u00b2 - 1) = (a\u00b2 - 1)(b\u00b2 - 1).\n\nWait, that seems right. Let me verify:\n\n(a\u00b2 - 1)(b\u00b2 - 1) = a\u00b2b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2 + 1. Yes, exactly! So the equation becomes:\n\n(a\u00b2 - 1)(b\u00b2 - 1) = 1.\n\nOh, interesting! So now, we have two integers (a\u00b2 - 1) and (b\u00b2 - 1) whose product is 1. Since we're dealing with integers, the pairs of integers that multiply to 1 are (1, 1) and (-1, -1). Let's write that down.\n\nSo either:\n\n1. a\u00b2 - 1 = 1 and b\u00b2 - 1 = 1, or\n2. a\u00b2 - 1 = -1 and b\u00b2 - 1 = -1.\n\nLet's analyze each case.\n\nCase 1: a\u00b2 - 1 = 1 \u21d2 a\u00b2 = 2 \u21d2 a = \u00b1\u221a2, but that's not an integer. So no solutions here.\n\nCase 2: a\u00b2 - 1 = -1 \u21d2 a\u00b2 = 0 \u21d2 a = 0. Similarly, b\u00b2 - 1 = -1 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 0 \u21d2 b = 0. But we already considered the (0, 0) solution earlier. So the only solution in integers is (0, 0).\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss any other cases. Because when you have two integers multiplying to 1, it's either (1,1), (-1, -1). Are there any other possibilities? For example, could (a\u00b2 -1) and (b\u00b2 -1) be fractions? But since a and b are integers, a\u00b2 and b\u00b2 are integers, so a\u00b2 -1 and b\u00b2 -1 must also be integers. Therefore, their product is 1, so they must be 1 and 1 or -1 and -1. So no other integer pairs multiply to 1.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is (0,0). But wait, let me check with some small integers to see if there are any other solutions.\n\nLet me try a = 1. Then the equation becomes 1\u00b2*b\u00b2 = 1\u00b2 + b\u00b2 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 1 + b\u00b2 \u21d2 0 = 1, which is impossible. Similarly, if a = -1, same thing. If a = 2, then (2)^2*b\u00b2 = (2)^2 + b\u00b2 \u21d2 4b\u00b2 = 4 + b\u00b2 \u21d2 3b\u00b2 = 4 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 4/3, which is not an integer. Similarly, a = 3: 9b\u00b2 = 9 + b\u00b2 \u21d2 8b\u00b2 = 9 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 9/8, nope. Similarly, if a = 1, we saw no solution. If a = -2, same as a=2. So none of these work.\n\nWhat if a or b is 0? Well, we already saw that if a = 0, then b must be 0, and vice versa. So (0,0) is the only solution.\n\nWait, but let me test (0,0). Plugging into the equation: 0\u00b2*0\u00b2 = 0\u00b2 + 0\u00b2 \u21d2 0 = 0, which works. So that's valid.\n\nIs there any other possible case where a or b is zero? If a is zero and b is non-zero, then the left side is 0 and the right side is b\u00b2. So unless b is zero, that can't happen. So indeed, only (0,0) works.\n\nBut let me think again. Suppose one of them is 1. Let me check a =1. Then the equation is 1*b\u00b2 = 1 + b\u00b2, which implies 0 = 1. So impossible. Similarly, a= -1, same result.\n\nWhat if a=0, b=1? Then left side is 0, right side is 1, which doesn't work. So yeah, no other solutions.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a way to parametrize the solutions? Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, starting from the original equation: a\u00b2b\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2.\n\nIf neither a nor b is zero, then we can divide both sides by a\u00b2b\u00b2:\n\n1 = 1/b\u00b2 + 1/a\u00b2.\n\nSo 1 is equal to the sum of the reciprocals of a\u00b2 and b\u00b2. Since a and b are integers, their reciprocals squared are positive fractions. So 1/b\u00b2 + 1/a\u00b2 = 1. Let's think about possible integer values of a and b that satisfy this.\n\nSince 1/a\u00b2 and 1/b\u00b2 must be positive rational numbers adding up to 1. Let's suppose that a and b are positive integers (since if they are negative, their squares are positive, so same result). So we can assume a, b \u22651.\n\nSo 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1. Let's try small integers.\n\nFor a=1: 1 + 1/b\u00b2 =1 \u21d2 1/b\u00b2=0 \u21d2 impossible.\n\na=2: 1/4 + 1/b\u00b2 =1 \u21d2 1/b\u00b2=3/4 \u21d2 b\u00b2=4/3 \u21d2 b is not integer.\n\na=3: 1/9 +1/b\u00b2=1 \u21d21/b\u00b2=8/9 \u21d2b\u00b2=9/8 \u21d2 no.\n\na=4: 1/16 +1/b\u00b2=1 \u21d21/b\u00b2=15/16 \u21d2b\u00b2=16/15 \u21d2nope.\n\nSimilarly, increasing a makes 1/a\u00b2 smaller, so 1/b\u00b2 has to be larger than 1 - 1/a\u00b2. But since a and b are at least 1, but a=1 is invalid. So perhaps no solutions here either.\n\nAlternatively, if a or b is 0, we get (0,0). So yeah, seems like (0,0) is the only solution.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's another way. Suppose that a and b are non-zero integers. Then from the original equation:\n\na\u00b2b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2 =0.\n\nLet me factor this differently. Maybe add 1 to both sides as before, but that led us to (a\u00b2 -1)(b\u00b2 -1)=1. Which gives only (0,0). Alternatively, maybe factor the original equation:\n\na\u00b2b\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2.\n\nDivide both sides by b\u00b2 (assuming b\u22600):\n\na\u00b2 = (a\u00b2)/b\u00b2 + 1.\n\nWait, not helpful. Alternatively, rearrange:\n\na\u00b2b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 = b\u00b2 \u21d2 a\u00b2(b\u00b2 -1) = b\u00b2.\n\nSo a\u00b2 = b\u00b2/(b\u00b2 -1). Since a\u00b2 must be a positive integer, so b\u00b2/(b\u00b2 -1) must be a positive integer. Let's write that as:\n\na\u00b2 = 1 + 1/(b\u00b2 -1).\n\nBecause b\u00b2/(b\u00b2 -1) = 1 + 1/(b\u00b2 -1). So a\u00b2 must be 1 + 1/(b\u00b2 -1). For this to be an integer, 1/(b\u00b2 -1) must be an integer. Therefore, b\u00b2 -1 must be \u00b11. Because 1/(b\u00b2 -1) is integer only if b\u00b2 -1 divides 1.\n\nSo b\u00b2 -1 = \u00b11.\n\nCase 1: b\u00b2 -1 =1 \u21d2 b\u00b2=2 \u21d2 b is not integer.\n\nCase 2: b\u00b2 -1 = -1 \u21d2 b\u00b2=0 \u21d2 b=0.\n\nThus, only possible if b=0. Similarly, if we do the same for a, we get a=0. Hence, only solution is (0,0).\n\nTherefore, after going through different methods, it seems that the only integer solution is (0,0).\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe only integer solution is \\boxed{(0, 0)}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find all integer solutions \\((a, b)\\) of the equation \\(a^2 b^2 = a^2 + b^2\\), we start by considering different cases and rearranging the equation.\n\n1. **Case where \\(a = 0\\) or \\(b = 0\\)**:\n   - If \\(a = 0\\), the equation becomes \\(0 = b^2\\), implying \\(b = 0\\).\n   - Similarly, if \\(b = 0\\), the equation becomes \\(0 = a^2\\), implying \\(a = 0\\).\n   - Thus, \\((0, 0)\\) is a solution.\n\n2. **Case where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are non-zero integers**:\n   - Rearrange the equation: \\(a^2 b^2 - a^2 - b^2 = 0\\).\n   - Add 1 to both sides: \\(a^2 b^2 - a^2 - b^2 + 1 = 1\\).\n   - Factor the left-hand side: \\((a^2 - 1)(b^2 - 1) = 1\\).\n   - Since the product of two integers is 1, the pairs \\((a^2 - 1, b^2 - 1)\\) must be \\((1, 1)\\) or \\((-1, -1)\\).\n\n3. **Analyzing the factor pairs**:\n   - For \\((a^2 - 1) = 1\\) and \\((b^2 - 1) = 1\\), we get \\(a^2 = 2\\) and \\(b^2 = 2\\), which are not integers.\n   - For \\((a^2 - 1) = -1\\) and \\((b^2 - 1) = -1\\), we get \\(a^2 = 0\\) and \\(b^2 = 0\\), leading to \\(a = 0\\) and \\(b = 0\\).\n\n4. **Verification with small integers**:\n   - Trying values like \\(a = 1, -1, 2, -2, \\ldots\\) results in contradictions or non-integer solutions.\n\n5. **Alternative approach**:\n   - Divide the original equation by \\(a^2 b^2\\) to get \\(1 = \\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{b^2}\\).\n   - This implies the sum of reciprocals of squares of integers equals 1, which is only possible if both terms are zero, leading to \\(a = 0\\) and \\(b = 0\\).\n\nAfter thorough analysis and verification, the only integer solution is \\(\\boxed{(0, 0)}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Start with the given equation:\n   \\[\n   a^2 b^2 = a^2 + b^2\n   \\]\n\n2. Rearrange the equation to group all terms on one side:\n   \\[\n   a^2 b^2 - a^2 - b^2 = 0\n   \\]\n\n3. Add and subtract 1 to facilitate factoring:\n   \\[\n   a^2 b^2 - a^2 - b^2 + 1 - 1 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a^2 b^2 - a^2 - b^2 + 1 = 1\n   \\]\n\n4. Notice that the left side can be factored as a product:\n   \\[\n   (a^2 - 1)(b^2 - 1) = 1\n   \\]\n\n5. Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   (a^2 - 1)(b^2 - 1) = 1\n   \\]\n\n6. Since 1 is a product with integer factors, the pairs \\((a^2 - 1)\\) and \\((b^2 - 1)\\) can take the following values:\n   \\[\n   a^2 - 1 = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 - 1 = 1\n   \\]\n   or\n   \\[\n   a^2 - 1 = -1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 - 1 = -1\n   \\]\n\n7. Consider the first set of equations:\n   \\[\n   a^2 - 1 = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 - 1 = 1\n   \\]\n   Solving these:\n   \\[\n   a^2 = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 2\n   \\]\n   But \\(2\\) is not a perfect square, so there are no integer solutions here.\n\n8. Next, consider the second set of equations:\n   \\[\n   a^2 - 1 = -1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 - 1 = -1\n   \\]\n   Solving these:\n   \\[\n   a^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 0\n   \\]\n   Hence, we get:\n   \\[\n   a = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 0\n   \\]\n\n9. Therefore, the only solution is:\n   \\[\n   (a, b) = (0, 0)\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 0)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given a simple graph \\( G \\), let \\( \\Delta \\) be the maximum degree of a vertex. Prove that the chromatic number of \\( G \\) is at most \\( \\Delta+1 \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the chromatic number of a simple graph G is at most \u0394 + 1, where \u0394 is the maximum degree of a vertex in G. Hmm, chromatic number is the smallest number of colors needed to color the vertices such that no two adjacent vertices share the same color. Right. And \u0394 is the highest number of edges connected to any single vertex in the graph. \n\nLet me start by recalling some basics. I remember something about graph coloring and maybe there's a theorem related to this. Oh, right! This sounds like Brooks' Theorem. Wait, but Brooks' Theorem states that the chromatic number of a connected graph is at most \u0394 unless the graph is a complete graph or an odd cycle, in which case it's \u0394 + 1. But the question here is saying that in general, the chromatic number is at most \u0394 + 1 for any simple graph. So maybe Brooks' Theorem is a more specific result, but the general upper bound is \u0394 + 1, which is easier to prove?\n\nSo the problem here is to show that no matter what the graph is, you can always color it with \u0394 + 1 colors. That seems plausible. Let me think about how to approach this. Maybe induction? Or maybe a greedy coloring algorithm?\n\nI think the standard proof uses a greedy coloring approach. Let me recall how that works. If you order the vertices in