 the answer should be the family of lines x + y = k\u03c0 for all integers k. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is that the locus is the set of all lines satisfying x + y = k\u03c0 for some integer k. In boxed form, since they might want it expressed as a union, but in locus problems, often they just describe the set. So, perhaps writing it as the union over all integers k of the lines x + y = k\u03c0. But depending on the notation expected. \n\nAlternatively, in more descriptive terms: the locus consists of all points (x, y) lying on the lines x + y = k\u03c0 where k is any integer. So, the final answer is the union of these lines. \n\nI think that's thorough. I considered the sine function's zeros, checked the equation, verified with examples, considered the spacing between lines, and confirmed with test points. I don't see any mistakes in this reasoning.", "deepseek_solution": "The equation \\(\\sin(x + y) = 0\\) implies that \\(x + y\\) must be an integer multiple of \\(\\pi\\). This is because the sine function equals zero precisely at integer multiples of \\(\\pi\\): \\(\\sin(\\theta) = 0 \\iff \\theta = k\\pi\\) for some integer \\(k\\). Substituting \\(\\theta = x + y\\), we obtain:\n\n\\[\nx + y = k\\pi \\quad \\text{for some integer } k.\n\\]\n\nEach such equation represents a straight line in the \\(xy\\)-plane with slope \\(-1\\) and \\(y\\)-intercept \\(k\\pi\\). These lines are parallel and spaced vertically at intervals of \\(\\pi\\). For example:\n- When \\(k = 0\\), the line is \\(y = -x\\).\n- When \\(k = 1\\), the line is \\(y = -x + \\pi\\).\n- When \\(k = -1\\), the line is \\(y = -x - \\pi\\).\n\nThe locus of points \\((x, y)\\) satisfying \\(\\sin(x + y) = 0\\) is therefore the union of all such lines for every integer \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x + y = k\\pi \\text{ for some integer } k}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. We start by analyzing the given equation:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x + y) = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. Recall that the sine function is equal t