1} ( (2021 - k)/k )\n\nSimilarly, the RHS:\n\nRHS = sum_{m=1 to 21} [ (x - m)/(2021 - m) ]\n\n= sum_{m=1 to 21} [ x/(2021 - m) - m/(2021 - m) ]\n\n= x * sum_{m=1 to 21} [1/(2021 - m)] - sum_{m=1 to 21} [ m/(2021 - m) ]\n\nNote that in the RHS sum, the denominator 2021 - m goes from 2020 (when m=1) down to 2000 (when m=21). So if we let n = 2021 - m, then n goes from 2020 to 2000 as m goes from 1 to 21. Therefore, sum_{m=1 to 21} [1/(2021 - m)] = sum_{n=2000 to 2020} [1/n], but in reverse order. Since addition is commutative, the sum from n=2000 to 2020 is the same as from 2020 to 2000. However, wait, n would actually go from 2020 down to 2000 when m=1 to 21, so the sum is from n=2020 to n=2000, which is the same as from n=2000 to n=2020. So the sum over m=1 to 21 of 1/(2021 - m) is equal to sum_{n=2000}^{2020} 1/n.\n\nBut on the LHS, the sum of 1/k from k=1 to 21 is the harmonic series up to 21. So maybe the coefficients of x on both sides are different? Let's check.\n\nWait, the coefficient of x on LHS is sum_{k=1 to 21} 1/k, and on RHS, sum_{n=2000 to 2020} 1/n. So unless these two sums are equal, which seems unlikely, the coefficients of x would be different, leading to a unique solution for x.\n\nSimilarly, the constants terms (the terms without x) would be sum_{k=1 to 21} (2021 - k)/k on the LHS and sum_{m=1 to 21} m/(2021 - m) on the RHS. So if we can compute these sums, then we can write the equation as:\n\nx * [sum_LHS - sum_RHS] = [constant_term_RHS - constant_term_LHS]\n\nTherefore, x would be [constant_term_RHS - constant_term_LHS] / [sum_LHS - sum_RHS]\n\nBut before jumping into calculations, let's see if there's any symmetry or relationship between the sums that can simplify this.\n\nFirst, notice that the denominators on the RHS (2000 to 2020) are exactly 2000 more than the denominators on the LHS (1 to 21). Wait, 2000 is much larger. Hmm. Let me check:\n\nWait, denominators on the LHS are 1, 2, ..., 21, and denominators on the RHS are 2020, 2019, ...,