a +9}{4(4a^2 + 3)}\\)\n\nThis seems complicated. Maybe simplify step by step.\n\nFirst, write \\(h^2\\) as:\n\n\\(h^2 = \\frac{4(4a^2 + 3)(a^2 - a +1) - (16a^4 -16a^3 +28a^2 -12a +9)}{4(4a^2 +3)}\\)\n\nLet me compute the numerator:\n\nFirst term: \\(4(4a^2 + 3)(a^2 - a +1)\\)\n\nExpand this:\n\nFirst compute \\((4a^2 +3)(a^2 -a +1)\\):\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\n\\(4a^2 \\times a^2 = 4a^4\\)\n\n\\(4a^2 \\times (-a) = -4a^3\\)\n\n\\(4a^2 \\times 1 = 4a^2\\)\n\n\\(3 \\times a^2 = 3a^2\\)\n\n\\(3 \\times (-a) = -3a\\)\n\n\\(3 \\times 1 = 3\\)\n\nCombine:\n\n\\(4a^4 -4a^3 +4a^2 +3a^2 -3a +3 = 4a^4 -4a^3 +7a^2 -3a +3\\)\n\nMultiply by 4:\n\n\\(16a^4 -16a^3 +28a^2 -12a +12\\)\n\nSecond term: subtract the numerator of \\(x^2\\), which is \\(16a^4 -16a^3 +28a^2 -12a +9\\)\n\nSo total numerator:\n\n\\( [16a^4 -16a^3 +28a^2 -12a +12] - [16a^4 -16a^3 +28a^2 -12a +9] \\)\n\nSubtract term by term:\n\n16a^4 -16a^4 = 0\n\n-16a^3 +16a^3 = 0\n\n28a^2 -28a^2 = 0\n\n-12a +12a = 0\n\n12 -9 = 3\n\nTherefore, numerator is 3, denominator is \\(4(4a^2 +3)\\). Thus,\n\n\\(h^2 = \\frac{3}{4(4a^2 +3)}\\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\(h = \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4(4a^2 +3)}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{4a^2 +3}}\\)\n\nSo, the height corresponding to the base \\(C\\) is \\(h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{4a^2 +3}}\\)\n\nNow, we need to show that this height \\(h \\leq \\frac{1}{2}\\). Let's compute:\n\n\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{4a^2 +3}} \\leq \\frac{1}{2}\\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{4a^2 +3}} \\leq 1\\)\n\nWhich is equivalent to:\n\n\\(\\sqrt{3} \\leq \\sqrt{4a^2 +3}\\)\n\nSquare both sides:\n\n\\(3 \\leq 4a^2 +3\\)\n\nSubtract 3:\n\n\\(0 \\leq 4a^2\\)\n\nWhich is always true since \\(a\\) is real. Thus, the inequality holds for all real \\(a\\), and equality occurs when \\(4a^2 = 0\\) i.e., \\(a = 0\\). Therefore, the height \\(h\\) corresponding to the base \\(C\\) is always \\(\\leq \\frac{1}{2}\\), with equality when \\(a = 0\\).\n\nTherefore, the triangle has a height