boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve this problem, we will analyze the triangle with sides \\(\\sqrt{a^2 - a + 1}\\), \\(\\sqrt{a^2 + a + 1}\\), and \\(\\sqrt{4a^2 + 3}\\), where \\(a\\) is a real number, and prove that the height corresponding to the largest side of the triangle is at most \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n#### Step 1: Identify the largest side\nSince for any real number \\(a\\), the following inequalities hold:\n\\[ 4a^2 + 3 > a^2 + a + 1 \\]\nand\n\\[ 4a^2 + 3 > a^2 - a + 1, \\]\nwe can conclude that the largest side of the triangle is \\(\\sqrt{4a^2 + 3}\\).\n\n#### Step 2: Express the height corresponding to the largest side\nLet us denote the height corresponding to the side \\(\\sqrt{4a^2 + 3}\\) by \\(k\\). Our goal is to show that \\(k \\leq \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nIn a triangle, the angles opposite the largest side are always acute. Thus, the foot of the height corresponding to the largest side will always lie inside that side. Denote the segments into which the height divides the largest side as \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\) respectively. Hence, we have:\n\\[ x_1 + x_2 = \\sqrt{4a^2 + 3}. \\]\n\n#### Step 3: Use the Pythagorean theorem\nIn the right triangles formed by the height:\n\\[ x_1^2 + k^2 = a^2 - a + 1 \\]\nand\n\\[ x_2^2 + k^2 = a^2 + a + 1. \\]\n\n#### Step 4: Add the two equations\nBy adding the two equations above, we obtain:\n\\[ x_1^2 + x_2^2 + 2k^2 = 2a^2 + 2. \\]\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[ 2k^2 = 2a^2 + 2 - (x_1^2 + x_2^2). \\]\n\n#### Step 5: Simplify \\(x_1^2 + x_2^2\\)\nNotice:\n\\[ x_1^2 + x_2^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\left((x_1 + x_2)^2 + (x_1 - x_2)^2\\right). \\]\nThus:\n\\[ x_1^2 + x_2^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( (\\sqrt{4a^2 + 3})^2 + \\left(\\frac{2a}{\\sqrt{4a^2 + 3}}\\right)^2 \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(4a^2 + 3 + \\frac{4a^2}{4a^2 + 3}\\right). \\]\n\n#### Step 6: Substitute and simplify the expressions\nNow we substitute back:\n\\[ 2k^2 = 2a^2 + 2 - \\left(2a^2 + \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{2a^2}{4a^2 + 3}\\ri