\u221a2 cos\u03b8, \u221a2/2 cos\u03b8 - \u221a6/2 sin\u03b8, 0) - min(\u221a2 cos\u03b8, \u221a2/2 cos\u03b8 - \u221a6/2 sin\u03b8, 0)].\n\nThis expression is quite complex, but perhaps we can find its maximum by analyzing the function.\n\nLet me denote f(\u03b8) = max(\u221a2 cos\u03b8, \u221a2/2 cos\u03b8 - \u221a6/2 sin\u03b8, 0) - min(\u221a2 cos\u03b8, \u221a2/2 cos\u03b8 - \u221a6/2 sin\u03b8, 0)\n\nWe need to maximize f(\u03b8).\n\nThis function represents the total vertical range covered by the projections of C and D onto the y-axis.\n\nTo simplify, consider two cases:\n\nCase 1: Both y_C' and y_D' are positive.\n\nThen f(\u03b8) = max(y_C', y_D') - 0 = max(y_C', y_D')\n\nCase 2: One of y_C' or y_D' is positive, and the other is negative.\n\nThen f(\u03b8) = max(y_C', y_D') - min(y_C', y_D') = y_C' - y_D' (if y_C' > y_D')\n\nCase 3: Both y_C' and y_D' are negative.\n\nThen f(\u03b8) = 0 - min(y_C', y_D') = -min(y_C', y_D')\n\nBut since we are looking for the maximum area, it's likely achieved when the vertical span is maximized. Let's analyze these cases.\n\nFirst, find when y_C' and y_D' are positive:\n\ny_C' = \u221a2 cos\u03b8 > 0 => cos\u03b8 > 0\n\ny_D' = \u221a2/2 cos\u03b8 - \u221a6/2 sin\u03b8 > 0\n\nLet's solve when y_D' > 0:\n\n\u221a2/2 cos\u03b8 - \u221a6/2 sin\u03b8 > 0\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n\u221a2 cos\u03b8 - \u221a6 sin\u03b8 > 0\n\nDivide both sides by \u221a2:\n\ncos\u03b8 - (\u221a6 / \u221a2) sin\u03b8 > 0\n\nSimplify \u221a6 / \u221a2 = \u221a3\n\nThus:\n\ncos\u03b8 - \u221a3 sin\u03b8 > 0\n\nThis can be written as:\n\ncos\u03b8 > \u221a3 sin\u03b8\n\nDivide both sides by cos\u03b8 (assuming cos\u03b8 > 0):\n\n1 > \u221a3 tan\u03b8\n\ntan\u03b8 < 1/\u221a3\n\n\u03b8 < 30\u00b0 or \u03b8 > 330\u00b0 (since cos\u03b8 > 0 in Q1 and Q4)\n\nTherefore, in the interval \u03b8 \u2208 (-30\u00b0, 30\u00b0), y_D' > 0 when cos\u03b8 > 0.\n\nSo in this interval, both y_C' and y_D' are positive, so f(\u03b8) = max(y_C', y_D')\n\nWe need to