 draws:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Points from } (n(n-1)/2) \\text{ matches} = n-1.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Ensuring \\( n \\leq 11 \\):**\n   Combine points gained from matches, whether they result in wins or draws:\n   \\[\n   10n \\geq 2 \\cdot \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\implies 10n \\geq n(n-1) \\implies 10n \\geq n^2 - n.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solving Inequality:**\n   Solving the inequality \\( n^2 - 11n \\leq 0 \\) gives:\n   \\[\n   n(n - 11) \\leq 0,\n   \\]\n   so \\( n \\leq 11 \\).\n\n7. **Verification by Example:**\n   Consider \\( n = 11 \\). If 11 teams each earn exactly 10 points, we can construct the scenario:\n   - Each of the 11 teams can earn 1 point from each of the 10 other teams among themselves by drawing all 10 games.\n   - The remaining points can be accumulated from collaborating matches configuration for the rest of their points by losing to the other teams (thus earning 0 points in those games).\n\n8. **Conclusion:**\n   Thus, the maximum number of teams that can each earn exactly 10 points is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{11}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Without using a calculator, determine which of the numbers \\( 33^{12} \\), \\( 63^{10} \\), and \\( 127^{8} \\) is the smallest.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out which of these three numbers is the smallest: 33^12, 63^10, or 127^8. Hmm, without using a calculator, that's going to require some clever thinking. Let me start by recalling some strategies for comparing large exponents. Maybe taking logarithms would help because logarithms can turn exponents into multipliers, making them easier to compare. But since all the numbers are positive, their logarithms should preserve the order. Let me write down the three numbers again:\n\n1. 33^12\n2. 63^10\n3. 127^8\n\nSo, if I take the natural logarithm of each, then compare them, the smallest original number should correspond to the smallest logarithm. The natural logarithm of a^b