+1)(n +1)/6\n\nWhich is the formula we needed to verify:\n\n1\u00b2 +2\u00b2 +\u2026+n\u00b2= n(n +1)(2n +1)/6\n\nTherefore, part (d) is verified.", "deepseek_solution": "(a) The number of numbers in one table is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{n(n + 1)}{2}}\\).\n\n(b) Each number \\(k\\) appears \\(\\boxed{3k}\\) times in all three tables.\n\n(c) For each position \\((i, j)\\):\n- First table: \\(\\begin{cases} n - i + 1 & \\text{if } j \\leq n - i + 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}\\)\n- Second table: \\(\\begin{cases} n - j + 1 & \\text{if } i \\leq n - j + 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}\\)\n- Third table: \\(i + j - 1\\)\n- Resulting table: \\(\\begin{cases} 2n + 1 & \\text{if } i + j \\leq n + 1 \\\\ i + j - 1 & \\text{if } i + j > n + 1 \\end{cases}\\)\n\n(d) The verification confirms the identity:\n\\[1^{2} + 2^{2} + 3^{2} + \\ldots + n^{2} = \\frac{1}{6}n(n+1)(2n+1)\\]\n\\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}n(n+1)(2n+1)}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n(a) To determine the number of numbers in each table, consider the procedure of turning one table upside down and joining it with another to form a rectangle. This rectangle will have $n$ rows and $n + 1$ columns. Since this rectangle contains twice the number of numbers as a single table, we conclude that the number of numbers in one table is:\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n + 1)}{2}\n\\]\n\n(b) To find out how many times each number \\( k \\) appears in all three tables, note the following:\n\n- In the first table, each number \\( k = 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\), appears \\( k \\) times.\n- In the second table, each number \\( k \\) likewise appears \\( k \\) times.\n- In the third table, each number \\( k \\) also appears \\( k \\) times.\n\nTherefore, each number \\( k \\) appears:\n\\[\n3k \\text{ times in total}\n\\]\n\n(c) For any position, given by row \\( i \\) and column \\( j \\):\n\n- In the first table, each position in row \\( i \\) (where \\( i \\) starts from 1 and goes up to \\( n \\)) contains the number \\( n + 1 - i \\).\n- In the second table, each position in column \\( j \\) contains the number \\( n + 1 - j \\).\n- In the third table, each position \\( (i, j) \\) on the diagonal depends on the sum \\( i + j \\). The number at this position is \\( i + j - 1 \\).\n\nThus, combining these, in the resulting table, each position \\( (i, j) \\) contains:\n\\[\n(n + 1 - i) + (n + 1 - j) + (i + j - 1) = 2n + 1\n\\]\n\n(d) Using the observations from the previous parts, we see that the resultant table has \\( \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} \\) elements, and each element in the resultant table is equal to \\( 2n + 1 \\). Therefore, the sum of all the numbers in the three given tables can be calculated by multiplying the total number of elements by the value of each element in the resultant table:\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} \\times (2n + 1)\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, summing the numbers from each of the original three tables can be written as:\n\\[\n(3 \\cdot 1) \\cdot 1 + (3 \\cdot 2) \\cdot 2 + \\ldots + (3 \\cdot n) \\cdot n = 3 \\left(1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2 + \\ldots + n^2\\right)\n\\]\n\nEquating these two results:\n\\[\n3 \\left(1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2 + \\ldots + n^2\\right) = \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} (2n + 1)\n\\]\n\nDividing through by 3:\n\\[\n1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2 + \\ldots + n^2 = \\frac{1}{6} n (n + 1) (2n + 1)\n\\]\n\nThus, we have shown:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2 + \\ldots + (n - 1)^2 + n^2 = \\frac{1}{6} n (n + 1) (2n + 1)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Show that if $\\varphi(t)$ is the characteristic function of a random variable $\\xi$, then the Laplace transform of the variable $|\\xi|$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{E} e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|}=\\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\lambda \\varphi(t)}{\\lambda^{2}+t^{2}} d t, \\quad \\lambda>0\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that the Laplace transform of |\u03be|, where \u03be is a random variable with characteristic function \u03c6(t), is given by this integral involving \u03c6(t) and some terms with \u03bb and t. Let me start by recalling what the Laplace transform and the characteristic function are.\n\nThe Laplace transform of a non-negative random variable X is usually defined as E[e^{-\u03bbX}]. In this case, though, we have |\u03be|. Since |\u03be| is non-negative regardless of \u03be's distribution, the Laplace transform should be E[e^{-\u03bb|\u03be|}]. That makes sense. \n\nThe characteristic function \u03c6(t) of \u03be is defined as E[e^{it\u03be}]. So \u03c6(t) is essentially the Fourier transform of the probability distribution of \u03be. Now, I need to relate the Laplace transform of |\u03be| to the characteristic function of \u03be. Hmm. The integral given is over all real numbers t, involving \u03c6(t) multiplied by \u03bb/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2). Let me write that down again:\n\nE[e^{-\u03bb|\u03be|}] = (1/\u03c0) \u222b_{\u211d} [\u03bb\u03c6(t)] / (\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2) dt\n\nSo, how do I connect these two? Let's think about the Laplace transform of |\u03be|. That is, E[e^{-\u03bb|\u03be|}]. Since |\u03be| is non-negative, maybe I can express this expectation in terms of the distribution of \u03be. Let's denote the probability measure of \u03be as P(dx). Then,\n\nE[e^{-\u03bb|\u03be|}] = \u222b_{\u211d} e^{-\u03bb|x|} P(dx)\n\nBut \u03c6(t) = \u222b_{\u211d} e^{itx} P(dx). So, \u03c6(t) is the Fourier transform of P(dx). Now, how can I relate the integral of e^{-\u03bb|x|} with respect to P(dx) to the Fourier transform of P(dx)?\n\nPerhaps I can use some integral transform that relates e^{-\u03bb|x|} to an integral involving e^{itx}. Let me recall that the Laplace transform can sometimes be related to the Fourier transform. Alternatively, maybe I can express e^{-\u03bb|x|} as an integral involving something with e^{itx}, so that I can swap the order of integration.\n\nWait, here's a thought. Maybe e^{-\u03bb|x|} can be written as a Fourier transform or an inverse Fourier transform of some function. Let me check. The function e^{-\u03bb|x|} is a two-sided exponential, which is known to have a Fourier transform. Let me recall: the Fourier transform of e^{-\u03bb|x|} is 2\u03bb/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2). Let me verify that.\n\nYes, the Fourier transform of e^{-a|x|} is 2a/(a\u00b2 + t\u00b2). So if I take a = \u03bb, then the Fourier transform of e^{-\u03bb|x|} is 2\u03bb/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2). So, maybe I can use the inverse Fourier transform here. That is, e^{-\u03bb|x|} = (1/(2\u03c0)) \u222b_{\u211d} [2\u03bb/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)] e^{-itx} dt. Simplifying, that would be (\u03bb/\u03c0) \u222b_{\u211d} [1/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)] e^{-itx} dt.\n\nAlternatively, since Fourier transforms can be defined with different conventions, I should be careful. Let me confirm: The Fourier transform of f(x) = e^{-\u03bb|x|} is given by:\n\nF(t) = \u222b_{-\u221e}^\u221e e^{-\u03bb|x|} e^{-itx} dx = 2\u03bb/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)\n\nWait, that's if we use the definition of Fourier transform with e^{-itx}. So, if we have that, then the inverse Fourier transform would be:\n\nf(x) = (1/(2\u03c0)) \u222b_{-\u221e}^\u221e F(t) e^{itx} dt\n\nSo, substituting F(t):\n\ne^{-\u03bb|x|} = (1/(2\u03c0)) \u222b_{-\u221e}^\u221e [2\u03bb/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)] e^{itx} dt = (\u03bb/\u03c0) \u222b_{-\u221e}^\u221e [1/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)] e^{itx} dt\n\nOkay, so that gives an expression for e^{-\u03bb|x|} as an integral involving e^{itx} and 1/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2). Therefore, substituting this into the Laplace transform expectation:\n\nE[e^{-\u03bb|\u03be|}] = E[ (\u03bb/\u03c0) \u222b_{-\u221e}^\u221e [1/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)] e^{it\u03be} dt ]\n\nAssuming Fubini's theorem applies, we can swap the expectation and the integral:\n\n= (\u03bb/\u03c0) \u222b_{-\u221e}^\u221e [1/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)] E[e^{it\u03be}] dt\n\nBut E[e^{it\u03be}] is exactly the characteristic function \u03c6(t). Therefore,\n\n= (\u03bb/\u03c0) \u222b_{-\u221e}^\u221e [\u03c6(t)/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)] dt\n\nWhich is the same as the given formula:\n\n= (1/\u03c0) \u222b_{\u211d} [\u03bb\u03c6(t)]/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2) dt\n\nThus, the result is proved. Let me check the steps again to make sure I didn't skip anything important.\n\nFirst, I used the fact that e^{-\u03bb|x|} can be expressed via the inverse Fourier transform of 2\u03bb/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2). Then, substituted that into the expectation, which is an integral over x with respect to the distribution of \u03be. Swapped the order of integration using Fubini's theorem, which requires that the integrand is absolutely integrable. Let's verify that.\n\nThe integrand after swapping would be [\u03bb/\u03c0] [\u03c6(t)/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)]. Since |\u03c6(t)| \u2264 1 (because it's a characteristic function), the absolute value is \u2264 [\u03bb/\u03c0] [1/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2)]. The integral over \u211d of 1/(\u03bb\u00b2 + t\u00b2) dt is \u03c0/\u03bb, so the integral becomes [\u03bb/\u03c0] * [\u03c0/\u03bb] = 1, which is finite. Therefore, Fubini's theorem applies, and the swap is valid.\n\nTherefore, all steps are justified, and the formula holds.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe Laplace transform of \\( |\\xi| \\) is given by the formula \\boxed{ \\mathrm{E} e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|} = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\lambda \\varphi(t)}{\\lambda^{2}+t^{2}} d t }.", "deepseek_solution": "To show that the Laplace transform of \\( |\\xi| \\) is given by the formula \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{E} e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|} = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\lambda \\varphi(t)}{\\lambda^{2}+t^{2}} d t, \\quad \\lambda>0,\n$$\n\nwe start by noting that the Laplace transform of \\( |\\xi| \\) is \\( \\mathrm{E}[e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|}] \\). This can be expressed as an integral with respect to the probability measure \\( P(dx) \\) of \\( \\xi \\):\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{E}[e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|}] = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} e^{-\\lambda|x|} P(dx).\n$$\n\nThe characteristic function \\( \\varphi(t) \\) of \\( \\xi \\) is given by \\( \\varphi(t) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} e^{itx} P(dx) \\). To relate this to the Laplace transform, we use the Fourier transform of \\( e^{-\\lambda|x|} \\). The Fourier transform of \\( e^{-\\lambda|x|} \\) is known to be \\( \\frac{2\\lambda}{\\lambda^2 + t^2} \\). Using the inverse Fourier transform, we can write:\n\n$$\ne^{-\\lambda|x|} = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1}{\\lambda^2 + t^2} e^{itx} dt.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expectation expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{E}[e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|}] = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\left( \\frac{\\lambda}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1}{\\lambda^2 + t^2} e^{itx} dt \\right) P(dx).\n$$\n\nBy Fubini's theorem, we can swap the order of integration:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{E}[e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|}] = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1}{\\lambda^2 + t^2} \\left( \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} e^{itx} P(dx) \\right) dt.\n$$\n\nRecognizing that the inner integral is the characteristic function \\( \\varphi(t) \\), we have:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{E}[e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|}] = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\varphi(t)}{\\lambda^2 + t^2} dt.\n$$\n\nThus, the Laplace transform of \\( |\\xi| \\) is given by:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{ \\mathrm{E} e^{-\\lambda|\\xi|} = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\lambda \\varphi(t)}{\\lambda^{2}+t^{2}} d t }.\n$$", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\n1. **Introduce a new random variable \\(\\eta\\)**: \n   Let \\(\\eta\\) be a random variable that is independent of \\(\\xi\\) and follows the Cauchy distribution with the probability density function \\(f_\\eta(x) = \\frac{1}{\\pi (1 + x^2)}\\).\n   The characteristic function of \\(\\eta\\) is \\(e^{-|t|}\\).\n\n2. **Apply Fubini's theorem to compute the expectation**: \n   Begin by considering the Laplace transform of \\(|\\xi|\\):\n\n    $$\\mathrm{E} [e^{-\\lambda |\\xi|}]$$\n\n3. **Utilize the independence of \\(\\xi\\) and \\(\\eta\\)**:\n   To introduce \\(\\eta\\), note that the independence allows the expression:\n\n    \\[\n    \\mathrm{E} [e^{-\\lambda |\\xi|}] = \\mathrm{E} [e^{-\\lambda |\\xi| \\eta} e^{\\lambda |\\xi| (1 - \\eta)}]\n    \\]\n\n4. **Rewrite the expectation using the characteristic function \\(\\varphi\\)**:\n   Recognizing the independence and properties of \\(\\eta\\):\n\n    \\[\n    \\mathrm{E} [e^{-\\lambda |\\xi|}] = \\mathrm{E} [e^{i \\lambda \\xi \\eta}]\n    \\]\n\n   And since \\(\\eta\\) follows the Cauchy distribution with its characteristic function \\(e^{-|t|}\\):\n\n    \\[\n    \\mathrm{E} [e^{i \\lambda \\xi \\eta}] = \\mathrm{E} [\\varphi(\\lambda \\eta)]\n    \\]\n\n5. **Substitute the probability density function of \\(\\eta\\)**:\n   Use the fact that the density of the Cauchy distribution is \\(f_\\eta(t) = \\frac{1}{\\pi (1 + t^2)}\\), and substitute into the integral form of expectation:\n\n    \\[\n    \\mathrm{E} [\\varphi(\\lambda \\eta)] = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\varphi(\\lambda t) \\frac{1}{\\pi (1 + t^2)} \\, dt\n    \\]\n\n6. **Simplify the integral**:\n   Perform a change of variables \\(u = \\lambda t\\) to simplify the expression:\n\n    \\[\n    du = \\lambda \\, dt \\quad \\text{or} \\quad dt = \\frac{du}{\\lambda}\n    \\]\n   \n   Thus, the integral becomes:\n\n    \\[\n    \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\varphi(u) \\frac{1}{\\pi (1 + (u/\\lambda)^2)} \\frac{du}{\\lambda} = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\lambda \\varphi(u)}{\\lambda^2 + u^2} \\, du\n    \\]\n\n7. **Write the final expression**:\n\n    \\[\n    \\mathrm{E} [e^{-\\lambda |\\xi|}] = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\lambda \\varphi(t)}{\\lambda^2 + t^2} \\, dt\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{\\mathrm{E} [e^{-\\lambda |\\xi|}] = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\lambda \\varphi(t)}{\\lambda^2 + t^2} \\, dt, \\quad \\lambda > 0}\n    \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Remove all perfect squares and perfect cubes from