ct squares less than 2070.\n\n2. **Perfect cubes:**\n   - The largest perfect cube less than 2070 is \\( 12^3 \\):\n     \\[\n     12^3 = 1728\n     \\]\n   - The next perfect cube, \\( 13^3 \\), is greater than 2070:\n     \\[\n     13^3 = 2197\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, there are 12 perfect cubes less than 2070.\n\n3. **Perfect sixth powers:**\n   - A number is a perfect sixth power if it is both a perfect square and a perfect cube, i.e., of the form \\( (n^2)^3 = n^6 \\).\n   - The largest perfect sixth power less than 2070 is \\( 3^6 \\):\n     \\[\n     3^6 = 729\n     \\]\n   - The next perfect sixth power, \\( 4^6 \\), is greater than 2070:\n     \\[\n     4^6 = 4096\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, there are 3 perfect sixth powers less than 2070.\n\n#### Step 3: Calculating total numbers to remove and remaining numbers\n\n- Perfect sixth powers are already counted in both perfect squares and perfect cubes, so they need to be added back once after being subtracted twice.\n- Therefore, the total number of special numbers to be removed is:\n  \\[\n  45 + 12 - 3 = 54\n  \\]\n\n- The number of remaining elements in \\( A \\) up to 2070 is:\n  \\[\n  2070 - 54 = 2016\n  \\]\n\n#### Step 4: Identifying the 2014th element\n\nGiven that there are 2016 elements left and 2070 is part of those remaining:\n\n- The last three terms in this sequence are 2070, 2069, and 2068.\n- Since 2070 is the 2016th element, moving two positions back gives us:\n  \\[\n  \\text{2016th element: } 2070\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\text{2015th element: } 2069\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\text{2014th element: } 2068\n  \\]\n\n#### Conclusion:\n\nHence, the 2014th term in the sequence is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2068}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\\( p(z) \\) is a polynomial of degree \\( n \\) with complex coefficients. Its roots (in the complex plane) can be covered by a disk of radius \\( r \\). Show that for any complex \\( k \\), the roots of \\( 