s of the chessboard.\n2. Each rectangle contains an equal number of black and white squares.\n3. If the number of white squares in the $i$-th rectangle is $a_i$, then $a_1 < a_2 < \\cdots < a_p$.\n\nFind the maximum possible value of $p$. Additionally, list all possible sequences $a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_p$ for this maximum $p$.\n\n\n1. Since each rectangle must contain an equal number of black and white squares, for any valid rectangle, the number of total squares must be even. Hence, the minimum number of squares in any rectangle is 2 (i.e., 1 white and 1 black square).\n\n2. Since $a_i$ is the number of white squares, the total number of squares (both black and white) in each rectangle is $2a_i$. The total number of squares in the chessboard is $64$.\n\n3. Given $a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_p = 32$ (since each $a_i$ represents half the number of squares in that rectangle, and there are 64 squares in total).\n\n4. For sequences $a_i$ to form a valid partition of 32, we need to ensure each $a_i \\geq i$ (condition 3). Therefore, the sum of the first $p$ integers (as each $a_i \\geq i$) should be less than or equal to 32:\n   \\[\n   1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + p \\leq 32\n   \\]\n   This is the sum of the first $p$ integers:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p(p + 1)}{2} \\leq 32\n   \\]\n   \n5. Solving for $p$ involves finding the largest integer $p$ satisfying this inequality:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p(p + 1)}{2} \\leq 32\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   p(p + 1) \\leq 64\n   \\]\n\n   Checking values for $p$:\n   - For $p = 7$,\n     \\[\n     7 \\cdot (7 + 1) = 56 \\quad (\\leq 64)\n     \\]\n     is true.\n   - For $p = 8$,\n     \\[\n     8 \\cdot (8 + 1) = 72 \\quad (\\not\\leq 64)\n     \\]\n     is false.\n\n   Therefore, the maximum value of $p$ such that $p(p + 1) \\leq 64$ is 7.\n\n6. Next, we need to form sequences where:\n   \\[\n   a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_7 = 32\n   \\]\n   and each $a_i \\geq i$. This ensures $a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_7$ are distinct and increasing:\n\n   - The minimum sum of (1, 