_2 \\), Petya covers 900 meters:  \n     \\[\n     V_p \\cdot t_2 = 900 \\implies V_p = \\frac{900}{t_2} = \\frac{900}{\\frac{1000}{V_d}} = \\frac{9}{10}V_d.\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\( V_d = 0.9V_m \\):  \n     \\[\n     V_p = \\frac{9}{10}(0.9V_m) = 0.81V_m.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Distance Between Petya and Dima When Misha Finishes:**\n   - At time \\( t_1 \\), Dima has traveled \\( 900 \\) meters.\n   - At time \\( t_1 \\), Petya's distance is:  \n     \\[\n     V_p \\cdot t_1 = 0.81V_m \\cdot \\frac{1000}{V_m} = 810 \\text{ meters}.\n     \\]\n   - The distance between Dima (900 meters) and Petya (810 meters) is:  \n     \\[\n     900 - 810 = 90 \\text{ meters}.\n     \\]\n\n**Answer:**  \nThe distance between Petya and Dima when Misha finished was \\(\\boxed{90}\\) meters.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify the speeds of each cyclist**:\n   \n   Let $v_1$ be the speed of Misha, $v_2$ be the speed of Dima, and $v_3$ be the speed of Petya.\n\n2. **Relationship between Misha and Dima's speeds**:\n   \n   Given that Dima still had 0.1 km left to go when Misha finished the 1 km race, we have:\n   \n   \\[\n   v_2 = 0.9 v_1\n   \\]\n\n   This is because during the time Misha covered 1 km, Dima covered only 0.9 km. \n\n3. **Relationship between Dima and Petya's speeds**:\n   \n   Given that Petya still had 0.1 km left to go when Dima finished the 1 km race, we have:\n\n   \\[\n   v_3 = 0.9 v_2\n   \\]\n   \n   Substituting $v_2$ from the previous relationship, we get:\n\n   \\[\n   v_3 = 0.9 \\times 0.9 v_1 = 0.81 v_1\n   \\]\n\n   So, Petya's speed is:\n\n   \\[\n   v_3 = 0.81 v_1\n   \\]\n\n4. **Distance traveled by Petya when Misha finishes**:\n   \n   When Misha finishes the race, he has traveled 1 km (1000 meters). In the same time $t$, Dima has traveled:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance traveled by Dima} = v_2 \\cdot t = (0.9 v_1) \\cdot t = 0.9 \\cdot 1000 = 900 \\text{ meters}\n   \\]\n\n   Similarly, in the same time $t$, Petya has traveled:\n\n   \\[\n