ominators are \\( S_x \\), \\( S_y \\), and \\( S_x + S_y \\). The common denominator would be \\( S_x S_y (S_x + S_y) \\).\n\nExpress each term:\n\n\\( \\frac{Q_x}{S_x} = \\frac{Q_x S_y (S_x + S_y)}{S_x S_y (S_x + S_y)} \\)\n\n\\( \\frac{Q_y}{S_y} = \\frac{Q_y S_x (S_x + S_y)}{S_x S_y (S_x + S_y)} \\)\n\n\\( \\frac{Q_x + Q_y + 2\\sum x_i y_i}{S_x + S_y} = \\frac{(Q_x + Q_y + 2\\sum x_i y_i) S_x S_y}{S_x S_y (S_x + S_y)} \\)\n\nTherefore, the difference is:\n\n\\( \\frac{Q_x S_y (S_x + S_y) + Q_y S_x (S_x + S_y) - (Q_x + Q_y + 2\\sum x_i y_i) S_x S_y}{S_x S_y (S_x + S_y)} \\geq 0 \\)\n\nSince the denominator is positive, the inequality reduces to the numerator being non-negative:\n\n\\( Q_x S_y (S_x + S_y) + Q_y S_x (S_x + S_y) - (Q_x + Q_y + 2\\sum x_i y_i) S_x S_y \\geq 0 \\)\n\nExpand the terms:\n\nFirst term: \\( Q_x S_y S_x + Q_x S_y^2 \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( Q_y S_x^2 + Q_y S_x S_y \\)\n\nThird term: \\( - Q_x S_x S_y - Q_y S_x S_y - 2 \\sum x_i y_i S_x S_y \\)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n- \\( Q_x S_y S_x \\) cancels with \\( - Q_x S_x S_y \\)\n- Similarly, \\( Q_y S_x S_y \\) cancels with \\( - Q_y S_x S_y \\)\n- Left with \\( Q_x S_y^2 + Q_y S_x^2 - 2 \\sum x_i y_i S_x S_y \\geq 0 \\)\n\nSo we arrive back at the same inequality:\n\n\\( Q_x S_y^2 + Q_y S_x^2 \\geq 2 S_x S_y \\sum x_i y_i \\)\n\nWhich is the same as before. So we need to prove this.\n\nLet me consider this inequality again:\n\n\\( Q_x S_y^2 + Q_y S_x^2 \\geq 2 S_x S_y \\sum x_i y_i \\)\n\nLet me rearrange it:\n\n\\( Q_x S_y^2 + Q_y S_x^2 - 2 S_x S_y \\sum x_i y_i \\geq 0 \\)\n\nPerhaps factor this expression. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each term in the sum can be written as \\( x_i^2 S_y^2 + y_i^2 S_x^2 - 2 x_i y_i S_x S_y \\). Wait, but this is over each i. Wait, no. Wait, if I take \\( Q_x S_y^2 = \\sum x_i^2 S_y^2 \\), and similarly \\( Q_y S_x^2 = \\sum y_i^2 S_x^2 \\), and \\( 2 S_x S_y \\sum x_i y_i = 2 S_x S_y \\sum x_i y_i \\). So the entire expression is:\n\n\\( \\sum 