f node \\( a_i \\) must be the sum of its two children, which are either 0 or powers of 2.\n   - The root \\( a_1 \\) is fixed at 1024 (\\( 2^{10} \\)).\n\n2. **Recursive Formulation**:\n   - Define \\( f(n, k) \\) as the number of ways to assign values to a subtree of height \\( n \\) rooted at a node with value \\( 2^k \\).\n   - The recurrence relation for \\( f(n, k) \\) is derived based on three choices for each node: pass all to the left child, pass all to the right child, or split the value between both children.\n\n3. **Recurrence Relation**:\n   - For \\( n > 1 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(n, k) = 2 \\times f(n - 1, k) + [f(n - 1, k - 1)]^2 \\mod 100\n     \\]\n   - Base case: \\( f(1, k) = 1 \\) for all \\( k \\geq 0 \\).\n\n4. **Dynamic Programming Calculation**:\n   - Compute \\( f(n, k) \\) modulo 100 for increasing values of \\( n \\) and \\( k \\).\n   - The final result is \\( f(11, 10) \\mod 100 \\).\n\n5. **Result**:\n   - After computing the values step-by-step modulo 100, the final result for \\( f(11, 10) \\) is found to be 15.\n\nThus, the remainder when \\( X \\) is divided by 100 is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Define the Problem:**\n    - We need to determine the number of sequences \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_{2047} \\) satisfying:\n        1. Each \\( a_i \\) is either 0 or a power of 2.\n        2. \\( a_i = a_{2i} + a_{2i+1} \\) for \\( 1 \\leq i \\leq 1023 \\).\n        3. \\( a_1 = 1024 \\).\n\n2. **Visualize the Problem as a Binary Tree:**\n    - Consider the binary tree with \\( 2047 \\) nodes representing the sequence. Each non-leaf node \\( a_i \\) has two children: \\( a_{2i} \\) and \\( a_{2i+1} \\).\n    - The value at each node \\( a_i \\) for \\( 1 \\leq i \\leq 1023 \\) must equal the sum of its two children.\n\n3. **Recurrence Relation:**\n    - Define \\( f(p) \\) as the number of ways to fill a binary tree with \\( 2^p \\) leaves, where the root has value \\( 2^p \\).\n    - We seek \\( f(10) \\) because the tree has \\( 2^{10} = 1024 \\) leaves, and the root node \\( a_1 \\) totals \\( 2^{10} = 1024 \\).\n\n4. **Setting up the Recurrence:**\n    - We divide cases about the root value distribution:\n        - All value \\( 2^p \\) goes to one child (left or right): There are \\( f(p-1) \\) ways.\n        - Even split: Both children receive value \\( 2^{p-1} \\), so the total count is \\( f(p-1)^2 \\).\n    - The recurrence relation:\n    \\[\n    f(p) = 2 \\cdot f(p-1) + f(p-1)^2.\n    \\]\n\n5. **Base Case and Induction:**\n    - Base Case: \\( f(0) = 1 \\). If there are \\( 2^0 = 1 \\) leaf, there is exactly one node with value \\( 1 \\), satisfying the conditions trivially.\n    - Hypothesis: Assume \\( f(p-1) = 2^{2^{p-1}} - 1 \\).\n    - Inductive Step:\n    \\[\n    f(p) = 2 \\cdot (2^{2^{p-1}} - 1) + (2^{2^{p-1}} - 1)^2.\n    \\]\n\n6. **Calculation:**\n    - Expanding the inductive step:\n    \\[\n    f(p) = 2^{2^p} - 2 + \\left(2^{2^{p-1}} - 1\\right)^2.\n    \\]\n    - Simplify:\n    \\[\n    f(p) = 2^{2^p} - 2 + 2^{2^p} - 2 \\cdot 2^{2^{p-1}} + 1 = 2^{2^p} - 1.\n    \\]\n    - Verified that \\( f(p) = 2^{2^p} - 1 \\).\n\n7. **Final Calculation:**\n    - We need \\( f(10) \\):\n    \\[\n    f(10) = 2^{2^{10}} - 1 = 2^{1024} - 1.\n    \\]\n    - Find \\( 2^{1024} - 1 \\mod 100 \\).\n\n8. **Modular Arithmetic:**\n    - Note that \\( 2^{20} \\equiv 76 \\mod 100 \\).\n    - By Euler\u2019s Theorem \\( a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\mod n \\) where \\( \\phi(n) \\) is Euler's Totient function:\n    \\[\n    \\phi(100) = 40.\n    \\]\n    - Hence \\( 2^{40} \\equiv 1 \\mod 100 \\).\n    - Simplify \\( 2^{1024 \\mod 40} = 2^24 \\):\n    \\[\n    2^{24} = (2^{20}) \\cdot (2^4) = 76 \\cdot 16 = 1216.\n    \\]\n    - Thus:\n    \\[\n    2^{1024} \\equiv 1216 \\mod 100 \\equiv 16.\n    \\]\n    - Therefore:\n    \\[\n    2^{1024} - 1 \\equiv 16 - 1 \\equiv 15 \\mod 100.\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\na) Is it possible to load two coins so that the probabilities of landing on \"heads\" and \"tails\" are different, but the probabilities of landing on any of the combinations \"tails, tails\", \"heads, tails\", \"heads, heads\" are the same?\n\nb) Is it possible to load two dice so that the probability of landing on any sum from 2 to 12 is the same?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to tackle these two probability questions. I'll start with part a) first.\n\nSo, the question is asking if we can load two coins (meaning each coin has a different probability of landing heads or tails) such that the probabilities of getting heads or tails are different for each coin, but when we flip both coins, the probabilities of each combination (TT, HT, HH) are all the same. Hmm, that seems tricky. Let me break it down.\n\nFirst, let's denote the probabilities for each coin. Let\u2019s say the first coin has a probability of landing heads as p and tails as 1-p. The second coin might have a different probability, say q for heads and 1-q for tails. Since the coins are loaded, p and q don't have to be 0.5.\n\nNow, the combinations: TT, HT, HH. Wait, actually, when you flip two coins, there are four possible outcomes: HH, HT, TH, TT. But the question mentions three combinations. Wait, maybe they are considering HT and TH as the same? That is, maybe the order doesn't matter. So, if we consider unordered pairs, then the possible outcomes are {HH, HT, TT}. But in reality, HT can occur in two ways: HT or TH. So, if the problem is considering the combinations where order doesn't matter, then the probabilities would be:\n\n- P(HH) = p*q\n- P(HT) = p*(1 - q) + q*(1 - p)\n- P(TT) = (1 - p)*(1 - q)\n\nBut the problem states that the probabilities of landing on any of the combinations \"tails, tails\", \"heads, tails\", \"heads, heads\" are the same. So, all three of these should have equal probabilities. So, set each of these equal to 1/3 