, where x=sin10\u00b0. Solve for x: 4x^3 -3x +0.5=0. This is a cubic equation. Perhaps we can approximate the root.\n\nBut maybe this is complicating things. Alternatively, use known values. For example, sin15\u00b0\u22480.2588, sin0\u00b0=0, sin30\u00b0=0.5. Since sin\u03b8 is increasing in [0\u00b0,90\u00b0], sin10\u00b0 must be less than sin15\u00b0\u22480.2588. But 0.2588 is greater than 0.18, so this doesn't help.\n\nAlternatively, use a linear approximation. The derivative of sin\u03b8 at \u03b8=0 is 1. So the linear approximation is sin\u03b8\u2248\u03b8. But as we saw, sin\u03b8 <\u03b8, so we can use the integral test or concavity. Since sin\u03b8 is concave on [0,\u03c0], its graph lies below its tangent lines. Therefore, sin\u03b8 <\u03b8 for \u03b8>0. Hence, sin10\u00b0 <10\u00b0 in radians. 10\u00b0=\u03c0/18\u22480.1745<0.18. Hence, sin10\u00b0 <0.1745<0.18. Therefore, |a_100|=sin10\u00b0 <0.18.\n\nAlternatively, if we need a more precise bound, we can consider that sin\u03b8 <\u03b8 - \u03b8^3/6 + \u03b8^5/120. Wait, but actually, the Taylor series with remainder tells us that sin\u03b8 = \u03b8 - \u03b8^3/6 + R_5, where R_5 is the remainder. For the Maclaurin series of sin\u03b8, the remainder term after the \u03b8^3 term is R_5=\u03b8^5/120 cos(c) for some c between 0 and \u03b8. Since \u03b8=10\u00b0\u22480.1745 radians, then R_5=(0.1745)^5 /120 * cos(c). Since cos(c) \u22641, we have |R_5| \u2264(0.1745)^5 /120\u22480.000001325. So sin\u03b8=\u03b8 - \u03b8^3/6 + R_5. Therefore, sin\u03b8 <\u03b8 - \u03b8^3/6 + |R_5| <0.1745 -0.000885 +0.000001325\u22480.1736. Therefore, sin\u03b8\u22480.1736<0.18. Hence, the inequality holds.\n\nBut perhaps the simplest way is to note that 10\u00b0 in radians is \u03c0/18\u22480.1745, and since sin\u03b8 <\u03b8 for \u03b8>0, then sin10\u00b0<0.1745<0.18. Therefore, |a_100|=sin10\u00b0<0.18.\n\nAlternatively, if we need to do this without referencing radians, maybe use geometric arguments. For example, construct 