0 \\mod 360\\)\n- \\(10^2 \\equiv 100 \\mod 360\\)\n- \\(10^3 \\equiv 280 \\mod 360\\)\n- For \\(n \\geq 3\\), \\(10^n \\equiv 280 \\mod 360\\)\n\nThus, \\(10^{100} \\equiv 280 \\mod 360\\). The angle \\(280^\\circ\\) is in the fourth quadrant where cosine is positive. Therefore, \\(\\cos(280^\\circ) = \\cos(80^\\circ)\\) is positive.\n\n**Answer:** The sign of \\(a_{100}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\text{positive}}\\).\n\n### Part (b)\nTo prove \\(\\left|a_{100}\\right| < 0.18\\), we note \\(a_{100} = \\cos(280^\\circ) = \\cos(80^\\circ)\\). Converting \\(80^\\circ\\) to its equivalent sine form, \\(\\cos(80^\\circ) = \\sin(10^\\circ)\\). \n\nUsing the inequality \\(\\sin(\\theta) < \\theta\\) (in radians) for \\(0 < \\theta < \\pi/2\\):\n- Convert \\(10^\\circ\\) to radians: \\(10^\\circ = \\frac{\\pi}{18} \\approx 0.1745\\)\n- Since \\(\\sin(10^\\circ) < \\frac{\\pi}{18} \\approx 0.1745 < 0.18\\), we have \\(\\sin(10^\\circ) < 0.18\\)\n\nThus, \\(\\left|\\cos(80^\\circ)\\right| = \\cos(80^\\circ) = \\sin(10^\\circ) < 0.18\\).\n\n**Proof:** \\(\\left|a_{100}\\right| < 0.18\\) is proven.", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Determine the sign of $a_{100}$**:\n    - Given the sequence $a_{n}=\\cos \\left(10^{n}\\right)^{\\circ}$, notice that starting from $a_3$, the sequence stabilizes.\n    - For $n \\geq 3$:\n\n      \\[\n      10^{n+1} - 10^n = 10^n(10 - 1) = 9 \\cdot 10^n\n      \\]\n\n    - We observe that $9 \\cdot 10^n$ is a multiple of $360^\\circ$ for $n \\geq 3$. This shows that angles $10^n$ (for $n \\geq 3$) lead to equivalent angles modulo $360^\\circ$.\n    - Specifically, for $n = 100$:\n\n        \\[\n        10^{100} \\mod 360^\\circ = 1000^\\circ \\mod 360^\\circ = (360^\\circ \\cdot 3 - 80^\\circ) \\equiv 80^\\circ\n        \\]\n\n    - Since $\\cos 80^\\circ = \\cos (90^\\circ - 10^\\circ) = \\sin 10^\\circ$ and $\\sin 10^\\circ > 0$:\n\n      \\[\n      a_{100} = \\cos 80^\\circ = \\sin 10^\\circ > 0\n      \\]\n\n    Thus, the sign of $a_{100}$ is positive.\