]\n     and from this, \\( f(z) = (z + r) - r = z \\).\n\n   Thus, in all cases, \\( f(z) = z \\) for every real \\( z \\), which means \\( f(x) = x \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe two solutions to the functional equation are:\n\\[ \\boxed{f(x) = 0 \\text{ and } f(x) = x} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "If \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are coprime, and \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are natural numbers (\\(\\mathbf{N}\\)), then there exists a smallest \\( m = pq - p - q + 1 \\) such that for all \\( n \\geq m \\), \\( n \\) can be written as \\( n = px + qy \\) (where \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are non-negative integers).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out why if p and q are coprime natural numbers, then there's this smallest number m = pq - p - q + 1, and every number greater than or equal to m can be expressed as px + qy where x and y are non-negative integers. Hmm, this seems related to something called the Frobenius Coin Problem, right? I remember hearing about this in number theory, where you find the largest monetary amount that cannot be obtained using any combination of coins of specified denominations. In this case, the denominations are p and q, which are coprime. So the formula given here is supposed to be that largest number plus one, which would be the smallest number such that all numbers beyond it can be expressed as such combinations. Let me try to work through this step by step.\n\nFirst, since p and q are coprime, their greatest common divisor is 1. That\u2019s important because if they had a common divisor greater than 1, then any combination px + qy would also be divisible by that common divisor, which means numbers not divisible by it couldn't be expressed. But since they are coprime, they can generate all sufficiently large numbers. The key here is to find the threshold m beyond which all numbers can be expressed, and prove that m is indeed pq - p - q + 1.\n\nWait, but I 