ution is \u03b8 = 0. But let me check \u03b8 = \u03c0/6. Then sin\u03b8 = 1/2. The quadratic equation is x\u00b2 - (3*(1/2))x + (1/4) + 1 = x\u00b2 - (3/2)x + 5/4 = 0. Discriminant D = (9/4) - 5 = (9/4 - 20/4) = -11/4 < 0. So complex roots. Let's compute the roots:\n\nx = [3/2 \u00b1 i\u221a(11/4)]/2 = 3/4 \u00b1 i\u221a11/4. The modulus of each root is sqrt( (9/16) + (11/16) ) = sqrt(20/16) = sqrt(5)/2 \u2248 1.118. Therefore, |x\u2081| + |x\u2082| = 2*(sqrt(5)/2) = sqrt(5) \u2248 2.236 \u2260 2. So not satisfying the condition.\n\nAlternatively, \u03b8 where sin\u03b8 = 2/3, which is approximately 0.666. Then, discriminant D = 5*(4/9) -4 = 20/9 - 36/9 = -16/9 <0. So complex roots. Then, quadratic equation: x\u00b2 - 2x + (4/9 +1) = x\u00b2 - 2x + 13/9 =0. Roots: [2 \u00b1 i\u221a( (4 - 52/9 ) ] /2 = [2 \u00b1 i\u221a( (36/9 -52/9) ) ]/2 = [2 \u00b1 i\u221a(-16/9)]/2 = [2 \u00b1 i*(4/3)]/2 = 1 \u00b1 (2/3)i. The modulus is sqrt(1 + (4/9)) = sqrt(13/9) = sqrt(13)/3 \u2248 1.201. So sum of moduli \u2248 2.402 \u2248 2.4 \u22602. So doesn't satisfy.\n\nWait, so maybe \u03b8 =0 is the only solution? But let me check \u03b8 = \u03c0. But \u03b8 is in [0, \u03c0), so \u03c0 is excluded. If \u03b8 approaches \u03c0, sin\u03b8 approaches 0 from the positive side. Then the quadratic equation approaches x\u00b2 +1=0, same as \u03b8=0. But \u03b8=\u03c0 is excluded.\n\nBut wait, \u03b8=0 gives sin\u03b8=0, quadratic equation x\u00b2 +1=0, roots \u00b1i, which have modulus 1 each, sum 2. Correct. Any other \u03b8 where modulus sum is 2?\n\nWait, perhaps there's a mistake in assuming that complex roots must have product equal to 1. Wait, Vieta's formula gives x\u2081x\u2082 = sin\u00b2\u03b8 +1. If the roots are complex conjugates, then x\u2081x\u2082 = |x\u2081|\u00b2. Since |x\u2081| =1, then |x\u2081|\u00b2 =1, so sin\u00b2\u03b8 +1 =1, leading to sin\u03b8=0. That's the case. So unless the product x\u2081x\u2082 is allowed to be something else.\n\nWait, but if the roots are